Provision a person's SSH public keys.

kanidm can serve a person's authorized_keys to any host that asks
(`kanidm_ssh_authorizedkeys`, wired by nixpkgs' `services.kanidm.unix
.sshIntegration`), which is what lets someone log in to a machine that has no
local account for them and no key in its own config. What it cannot do is learn
those keys: upstream kanidm-provision's `persons.<n>` carries `displayName`,
`legalName` and `mailAddresses` and nothing else, so the keys had to be put
there by hand -- once per person, per key, forever.

This adds `persons.<name>.sshPublicKeys = { <tag> = "<key>"; }`.

WHY NOT THROUGH `update_attrs!`, which every other person attribute uses:
kanidm's `ssh_publickey` is a (tag, key) PAIR and the generic `_attr` endpoint
has no way to carry the tag. The dedicated `/_ssh_pubkeys` endpoint is the only
one that can express it, so the sync is written out rather than declared in the
macro.

RECONCILED, NOT APPENDED. Every declared tag is POSTed -- which REPLACES a key
of the same tag, so a rotated key needs no special case -- and every tag kanidm
holds that is no longer declared is deleted. A key removed from the declaration
is therefore removed from the person, which is the difference between a
provisioned set and an append-only one, and it is what makes revocation work.

The tag parsing in `list_ssh_pubkey_tags` reads kanidm's own rendering of an ssh
key value, `tag: ssh-... comment` (`to_proto_string_clone` in
`valueset_sshkey.rs`), and takes the part before the first colon.

Pairs with ./application-passwords.patch and ./service-accounts.patch and is
generated against the tree with both already applied.

--- a/src/state.rs
+++ b/src/state.rs
@@ -27,6 +27,16 @@
     /// own model -- so it is the key rather than a field.
     #[serde(default)]
     pub application_passwords: HashMap<String, ApplicationPassword>,
+    /// SSH public keys for this person, keyed by TAG. kanidm stores an ssh key
+    /// as a (tag, key) pair and addresses it by tag, so the tag is the key of
+    /// this map rather than a field -- the same reasoning `application_passwords`
+    /// above uses for its label.
+    ///
+    /// These are PUBLIC keys, so unlike an application password the value is
+    /// the material itself and not a path to it: there is nothing here to keep
+    /// out of the state JSON.
+    #[serde(default)]
+    pub ssh_public_keys: HashMap<String, String>,
 }
 
